<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip III of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1245–<a href="/years/1285">1285</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Philip the Bold · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip III, called the Bold, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1270 to 1285, the son of Louis IX. He inherited the lands of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and in 1274 he handed the Comtat Venaissin, which had passed to the French crown after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-x">Pope Gregory X</a>. He died in 1285 after a failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip III of France" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_III_of_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born in Poissy on 1 May 1245, the second son of King Louis IX and Margaret of Provence. He became heir to the throne when his older brother Louis died in 1260. In 1262 he was married to Isabella of Aragon, in line with the Treaty of Corbeil, and his father knighted him on 5 June 1267. Louis IX also wrote the Enseignements for him, which presented justice as the first duty of a king.</p><p>Philip accompanied his father on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1270. An epidemic of dysentery struck the army after it took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, killing Philip's brother John Tristan on 3 August and Louis IX on 25 August. Philip, aged 25 and himself ill, was proclaimed king in Tunis. His uncle Charles I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> negotiated a treaty with the Hafsid caliph of Tunis on 5 November 1270. Philip's wife Isabella died after a fall from her horse while pregnant. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 15 August 1271.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Philip kept most of his father's domestic policies, including the ordinances against seigneurial warfare, which he reinforced in October 1274. On 21 August 1271 his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse, Count of Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, died childless, and Philip united his lands with the royal domain. In 1272 he campaigned against Roger-Bernard III, Count of Foix, who had invaded the County of Toulouse; the count surrendered and was imprisoned for a year, then freed. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> of 1275 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> by arranging a marriage between one of his sons and the heiress Joan, and French governors were sent there. A revolt in 1276 was put down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-count-of-artois">Robert II, Count of Artois</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> of 1282 Pope Martin IV granted Aragon to Philip's son Charles, and Philip launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aragonese-crusade">Aragonese Crusade</a>, at the urging of his wife Marie of Brabant and of Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> on 7 September 1285, but dysentery struck the camp. The retreating French were defeated at the Col de Panissars on 1 October, and Philip died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a> on 5 October 1285. His son Philip IV succeeded him. His reign added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, Auvergne, Guînes and Alençon to the royal domain, though the failed war nearly bankrupted the monarchy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip III of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The admiral Roger of Lauria won a series of victories against the Angevins and the French, and when <b>Philip III of France</b> invaded Catalonia in the Aragonese Crusade of 1285, the French army was forced to retreat after its fleet was destroyed at the Battle of Les Formigues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Paris of 1229 after the Albigensian Crusade, Raymond VII ceded it to the Church, but the French crown held it until <b>Philip III</b> handed it to Pope Gregory X in 1274.</blockquote><a href="/state/comtat-venaissin">Comtat Venaissin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Poissy Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.93,2.04&amp;year=1271" aria-label="Poissy Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4597 456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philip III of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5047 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5047 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.93,2.04&amp;year=1271">What was here in 1271? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
